               "opportunityCheck":  "Exposure looks right: AI was about 71% of Q1 revenue, and management expects 70–80% for FY27. The market-growth score fits the raised guidance, and the low bottleneck score fits a 12% gross margin. I would lower the position score. One customer now makes up 44% of sales, and repeated export-control investigations could make cautious hyperscalers, government buyers and Nvidia itself hesitant to rely on Supermicro. Optionality looks fair. If anything, the overall score leans slightly high because legal risk is not captured anywhere.",
               "technical":  "The trend is clearly down. Price ($21.65) sits about a third below all three EMAs (exponential moving averages, which track the trend): EMA50 $32.17, EMA100 $33.38, EMA200 $33.89. Their order (50 below 100 below 200) is the textbook downtrend setup. Momentum is negative. MACD (a trend-momentum gauge) is at −3.31, below its signal line at −2.85, and the histogram is −0.46, so selling pressure is still building rather than fading. RSI 14 (a 0–100 momentum gauge where under 30 means oversold) reads 28.04, which is oversold. Price is close to the lower Bollinger Band at $21.05, the bottom of the band that holds most of a stock\u0027s normal price swings. The 52-week range is $19.48–51.40, so the stock is about 58% below its high and only $2.17 above its low. Volume of 48.3M was just 4% above the 20-day average of 46.5M. The −9.19% drop on 20 Nov was not a panic flush. Support sits at $21.05 and then $19.48–20.53, the March lows. Resistance is near $27 (the 12 Nov close) and $28.90 (the middle band). Oversold readings can bring a bounce, but they do not end a downtrend by themselves.",
               "fundamental":  "Supermicro assembles AI servers and liquid-cooled racks around Nvidia and AMD GPUs. It wins on getting new platforms to customers fast, not on owning scarce technology. Fiscal Q1 2027, the quarter to 30 Sep 2026 reported on 3 Nov, was a blowout. Sales were $19.9B, net income $1.59B and non-GAAP EPS $1.81. Management raised FY27 revenue guidance to $72–82B from $65–72B. The quality behind those numbers is weaker. About $2.3B of Q1 revenue was catch-up from delayed deployments. Gross margin was 12.7% and is guided lower. One customer, which Raymond James believes is SpaceX, supplied 44% of sales. Working capital consumed cash: operating cash flow was −$434M against $8.6B of debt. The stock trades at about 0.33x trailing sales (Trefis). That valuation shows the market discounting legal risk and how long these earnings can last.",
               "news":  [
                            "3 Nov 2026: Fiscal Q1 sales were $19.9B, EPS was $1.81 non-GAAP, and FY27 revenue guidance was raised to $72–82B. Q2 guidance is $19.5–21.5B. The company had pre-announced the revenue beat on 20 Oct.",
                            "4 Nov 2026: Shares fell toward a three-month low despite the beat. Analysts focused on the 44% customer concentration and gross-margin guidance of 11.8–12.2% for FY27. Raymond James raised its target to $65 and kept an Outperform rating.",
                            "12 Nov 2026: The stock fell 11.3% to $26.99 after Bloomberg and Semafor reported that the Manhattan US Attorney\u0027s office is examining whether entities linked to Alice Huang smuggled Supermicro hardware to China. Supermicro says it cooperated and that Megaspeed was never a customer. Singapore\u0027s Home Affairs ministry called Supermicro\u0027s cooperation \u0027limited.\u0027",
                            "12 Nov 2026: Analyst downgrades of Nvidia amid its leadership turmoil weighed on AI server stocks, which compounded Supermicro\u0027s fall.",
                            "16–20 Nov 2026: Selling continued for the rest of the period, including a 5% drop on 16 Nov. On 20 Nov the stock fell another 9.19%. A market recap tied that day\u0027s drop to reports of a congressional probe into AI chip exports. I could not confirm that any congressional inquiry targets Supermicro directly; Senate and House export scrutiny so far centres on Nvidia and Megaspeed."
                        ],
               "caution":  "This is the third export-control cloud in 2026. Co-founder Liaw was charged in March and his trial is set for March 2027. The company has a grand jury subpoena plus SEC and BIS inquiries, and now a reported DOJ probe. Any charge against the company, or any curb on its Nvidia allocation, would hit a business that keeps only about 12 cents of gross profit per dollar of sales. Revenue is concentrated in one customer, cash flow is negative and debt is $8.6B, so a single order slip could swing results sharply. The chart shows no sign of a bottom yet.",
               "opportunity":  "The business keeps delivering. Sales almost quadrupled year on year, FY27 guidance is $72–82B, and the company was among the first to ship Vera Rubin NVL72 racks. At $21.65 the market is pricing in a lot of legal damage: about 0.33x sales and only a few times annualized EPS guidance. If the probes end without charges against the company, the gap between earnings and price could narrow. The next hard data point is Q2 results, scheduled for 2 Feb 2027.",
